    "alert_id": "EX3_001",
    "title": "Uncontrolled hypothyroidism despite levothyroxine",
    "message": "Patient has TSH 12.4 mIU/L (target below 4.5) with low Free T4, indicating inadequate hypothyroidism treatment. She reports often forgetting the thyroid tablet, which is the likely primary cause. Symptoms include tiredness, feeling cold, constipation, dry skin, periorbital puffiness, and delayed ankle reflexes—all consistent with hypothyroidism. The clinician's plan to continue levothyroxine 50 mcg without addressing adherence or dose adjustment is insufficient. Uncontrolled hypothyroidism can also contribute to weight gain, hyperlipidemia, and low mood, compounding the DTG weight gain concern.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Counsel on levothyroxine adherence and escalate dose (e.g., 75–100 mcg). Recheck TSH in 6–8 weeks. Hypothyroidism may be contributing to weight gain and dyslipidemia.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX3_002",
    "title": "DTG weight gain attribution confounded by hypothyroidism",
    "message": "Guidelines note DTG is associated with weight gain and hyperglycemia risk, especially in patients with BMI ≥25. Patient gained 12 kg over 3 years on TLD with BMI now 33. However, uncontrolled hypothyroidism (TSH 12.4, low Free T4) is a significant alternative or contributing cause of weight gain, fatigue, and constipation. The plan to switch from DTG for weight gain should first address hypothyroidism, as weight gain may stabilize once thyroid function is corrected. Premature DTG switching risks losing the benefits of viral suppression and high genetic barrier.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Optimize hypothyroidism treatment first before attributing weight gain solely to DTG. Reassess weight after thyroid stabilization before deciding on ART switch.",
